<section id="html2rss" class="project__html2rss">
  <h2><span>HTML</span><span>2</span><span>RSS</span></h2>

  <p>
    My RSS reader is my favorite place on the Internet. Every interesting
    content from different websites appears in the same format. Well,&nbsp;theoretically.
    <br />
    The availability of RSS has declined while social networks became popular.
  </p>

  <p>
    <code>html2rss</code> scrapes websites to build RSS feeds, without
    reinventing the wheel for each and every website. With this tool I
    can continue to fill my favorite place on the Internet with interesting content.
  </p>

  <p>The <code>html2rss</code> project consists of three components:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <a href="https://github.com/html2rss/html2rss">
        <code>html2rss</code></a
      >: This ruby gem does the heavy lifting of scraping and feed building.
    </li>
    <li>
      <a href="https://github.com/html2rss/html2rss-web">
        <code>html2rss-web</code></a
      >: A web application which exposes the RSS feeds via HTTP.
    </li>
    <li>
      <a href="https://github.com/html2rss/html2rss-configs">
        <code>html2rss-configs</code></a
      >: A repository containing feed configs.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    The <code>config</code> and the <code>web</code> components follow a rolling
    release pattern. The <code>web</code> repository automatically updates when
    there are changes in the <code>config</code>
    repository and publishes a new version.
  </p>

  <p>
    Check out <a href="https://html2rss.github.io/">the project's website</a>.
  </p>

